                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "This is another game I had in my library for many years. \n\nRIVE looks great but plays awfully due to poor design. \n\nThe core issue with RIVE is that it's a shmup game fused with classic Megaman design, which sounds great as a concept but it doesn't work in practice when you have the same punishing nature as you would in a Megaman game combined with the speed of shmups. Unlike Megaman, RIVE feels like it only has 6 different enemies in total which come at you in the millions. \n\nI will admit that the gameplay bones are pretty good. The spider tank feels great to control. It's hyper-mobile, jumps land where you expect them to and is pretty powerful. \n\nThe slightest mistakes get brutally punished. There are way too many gotcha moments in terms of environmentals, enemy placement, event triggers or a combination of all three. Enemies or dangerous obstacles rushing you or you having to dodge from at moments where it always catches you off guard, often times far too late and impossible to avoid. \n\nBafflingly, this game is concerned with obscuring your vision constantly with explosions, dust, particle effects, muzzle flashes and so much more. It's very busy looking. The developers were far more interested in shiny graphics than clean graphics. \n\nThe field of view is way too zoomed in, exacerbating these issues even more and this also lends itself into the map design. Half of the game felt like a giant sewer level and often times I didn't know where to go due to some very bad signposting. Several times I had to look up a YouTube video several times to know where to go, which shouldn't be possible in a linear game but there we are. \n\nThe game's pretty inconsistent with oft-needed health- and ammo pickups. The frequency of when pickups appear seems very random. Some fights you'll have an abundance of drops, other fights you'll get none at all. This will lead to deaths that feel unfair because in other attempts pickups drop so frequently you have no chance of dying. The player isn't told what causes pickups to drop. This feels doubly unfair when you check the achievements list and see that the developers have the gall to ask you to complete the entire campaign in one run without dying, which is madness. \n\nThese elements combine to make for gameplay sessions with a start-and-stop feel and you never truly get into the flow of things. I have a very good sense when the level designer is fighting the player instead of working with them and I definitely feel it here. RIVE is mean-spirited, loaded with cruel traps and it loves punching down on you. It's not fun. \n\nA final but not unimportant note, the main character can not shut up for a minute. The writing comes from the Borderlands school of humor, which consists of constant fourth-wall breaking, quips and references to much better games and movies. It comes across as desperate. None of the \"please laugh at me!\" humor works and is years too late to the Borderlands party that was all the rage in the early 2010's.",
